description:
ui/mod.rs:1633 — format!("I ran: $ {}

Output:
{}", cmd, output) sent verbatim to agent. Attacker-controlled files (!cat downloads/foo.txt) carry prompt-injection text into the model. Fence output in delimited <tool_output> block + prepend 'treat as untrusted data' preamble. sanitize_output here only strips control chars for display, not LLM safety.

The crossterm input reader can spin at 100% CPU forever when the terminal dies, and dirge cannot interrupt it.

Upstream bug, crossterm 0.29 `UnixInternalEventSource::try_read`
(event/source/unix/mio.rs). The inner read loop is:

    loop {
        match self.tty_fd.read(&mut self.tty_buffer) {
            Ok(read_count) => { if read_count > 0 { parser.advance(...) } }
            Err(e) => {
                if e.kind() == WouldBlock { break }
                else if e.kind() == Interrupted { continue }
                // any other error falls through
            }
        };
        if let Some(event) = self.parser.next() { return Ok(Some(event)); }
    }

It breaks only on WouldBlock and retries on Interrupted. `Ok(0)` (EOF)
and any other errno (notably EIO) fall through, the parser yields
nothing, and the loop repeats with no timeout check. So `event::poll()`
never returns, even with a 1ms timeout.

Why it triggers: verified on macOS, an orphaned pty slave reports
POLLIN|POLLHUP forever and read() returns 0 forever. A dirge orphaned
into the background (parent reparented to launchd) also gets EIO
reading its controlling tty once its process group is orphaned.

Impact on dirge: src/ui/input_reader.rs drives that call in a loop. Its
`Err(_) => break` arm is unreachable because poll() never returns, and
the thread never re-checks EVENT_READER_SHUTDOWN. So the reader pins a
core, and terminal.rs's join_reader / suspend_tui_for_subprocess wait
barriers (~line 774) wedge behind a thread that will never exit.

This is the second spinner seen alongside the pty_relay tty-EOF bug
fixed in fix(pty-relay): stop the tty-EOF spin that pinned two cores.
That one is fixed; this one is still live.

Proposed fix: before calling `event::poll`, probe the same fd crossterm
uses (isatty(0) ? fd 0 : /dev/tty, opened once at reader start) with a
zero-timeout libc::poll. Treat POLLHUP|POLLERR|POLLNVAL as fatal and
break out of the reader loop. POLLHUP is not set on a live terminal, so
it is a safe discriminator and will not cause false exits.

Also worth reporting upstream to crossterm.

Open question, not covered by the guard: once the reader exits, the UI
loop keeps running with no input source. For an orphaned background
dirge the process still will not exit. Decide separately whether a dead
terminal should trigger shutdown.

comments.text:
FM-7 — a capability signal was wired into the estimator, unit-tested, and never fed.

Observed 2026-08-01 while auditing dirge-5mtx.7. GateTally::record_hallucinated_tool_name had ZERO production callers. Its only caller was this test:

    #[test]
    fn hallucinated_tool_names_are_counted() {
        let mut tally = GateTally::new();
        tally.record_hallucinated_tool_name();
        assert_eq!(tally.hallucinated_tool_names(), 1);
    }

which calls the recorder directly, so it passed while the counter was structurally always 0 in every real run. hallucinated_tool_names carries W_HALLUCINATED=2 in the tier formula, so a whole weighted signal could never contribute to a capability estimate. The estimator was blind to models inventing tool names, and worse, nobody could tell -- because the counter being zero is indistinguishable from the behaviour never happening.

Detection was a mechanical audit rather than an insight: for each 'pub fn record_*' on GateTally, count callers outside the module and its tests. Seven had callers, one had none. That check takes one shell loop and would have caught it the day it was written.

This is the same family as FM-3 ('the gate was green because it ran on a path the change does not affect') and FM-4 ('the measurement never confirmed the mechanism ran'), but on a PRODUCER rather than a consumer. The recurring shape across FM-3, FM-4, FM-6 and now FM-7 is: something was verified in isolation and never verified in situ.

MITIGATION (landed, d681648d): the classification is re-derived at the point the other counters are recorded, since prepare_tool_call detects the miss but sits inside the two batch executors and neither has the tally. Four tests drive the function the loop actually calls, not the recorder -- the distinction that mattered. A hallucinated call now counts as both errored and hallucinated, matching how repair_invalid already stacks.

GENERAL RULE worth carrying: a telemetry recorder with no production caller is dead code that looks alive, and a unit test that calls the recorder directly cannot tell the difference. Test the function the production path calls.
